\image(figures/generalizability_theory.svg)
// result_end

In this sample the persons component (0.82) is overestimated relative to its true value (0.5): with 30 people its standard error is 0.27. The other components are within about one standard error of the truth. Persons account for 44% of the variance and the residual for 36%. The raters, occasions and rater-by-occasion estimates rest on 1–2 degrees of freedom, and their standard errors are as large as the estimates. The D-study table shows how the error budget translates into design choices. One rating on one occasion would give \(E\rho^2=0.47\) here (true 0.37). Because \(\sigma^2_{po}\) exceeds \(\sigma^2_{pr}\), adding occasions helps a little more than adding raters: 1 rater × 6 occasions beats 6 raters × 1 occasion. \(\Phi\) is always below \(E\rho^2\), because absolute decisions also pay for rater leniency and occasion difficulty.

The repeated G-studies show how uncertain a D-study projection is. A G-study of 30 × 3 × 2, a common pilot size, predicts \(E\rho^2\) for the planned 2 × 4 design with an average of 0.73 (true 0.755). One study in five, however, falls outside 0.57–0.85: a pilot could as easily recommend extra raters as declare the design sufficient. Three G-studies in four produce at least one negative raw estimate, which is then truncated at 0. A 100 × 6 × 4 G-study narrows the range to 0.71–0.79, and negative estimates almost disappear (5%). The facet main effects and their interaction, estimated from 2–6 levels, are what keep \(\Phi\) uncertain.

>> Practical guidance

- Design the G-study with enough levels of each facet, at least 4–6 raters and occasions, not just enough persons. Variance components of facets with 2–3 levels are very imprecise, and they drive both \(\Phi\) and the D-study recommendations.
- Report all components with their standard errors and the raw negative estimates. Truncate at 0 only when computing coefficients.
- Use \(E\rho^2\) for norm-referenced decisions (ranking, correlational research) and \(\Phi\) for criterion-referenced ones (pass/fail against a fixed standard). Choose the number of raters and occasions by where the large person-by-facet components are.
- Treat D-study projections from small G-studies as rough, and confirm the chosen design with a larger study when stakes are high. Unbalanced or nested designs need REML variance components instead of the ANOVA solution.

>> References

- L. J. Cronbach, G. C. Gleser, H. Nanda and N. Rajaratnam (1972). *The Dependability of Behavioral Measurements: Theory of Generalizability for Scores and Profiles*. Wiley.
- R. L. Brennan (2001). *Generalizability Theory*. Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-3456-0
- R. J. Shavelson and N. M. Webb (1991). *Generalizability Theory: A Primer*. Sage.
- S. R. Searle, G. Casella and C. E. McCulloch (1992). *Variance Components*. Wiley. doi:10.1002/9780470316856
